# HEER ROUTE 101 — Cline Multi-Agent Factory Rules
# This file governs EVERY agent session. Read every line before doing anything.
# Last updated: 22 August 2026 — Master Principal Architect

## STEP 1 — Identify yourself and run the session guard

Your first action in every session, no exceptions:
  bash factory/session-guard.sh AGENT-XX
  (replace XX with your agent number)

Paste the complete output. If it fails: stop. Do not proceed.

## STEP 2 — Read your agent prompt

  cat factory/prompts/AGENT-XX.md

That file is your complete scope, your Owns list, your acceptance criteria.
Nothing outside it is authorized. Nothing inside it requires further approval
to plan — but everything requires Master approval before Act mode.

## STEP 3 — Establish baseline before touching anything

  git status
  git log --oneline -10
  docker-compose ps

Paste all three outputs in your Plan-mode summary.
Never assume the repo is in a known state. Always check.

## HARD RULES — never break these

1. Plan before Act. Always give Master a plan summary and wait for
   explicit approval before writing any file or running any command
   beyond the session guard and baseline checks above.

2. Own only your directories. Your Owns list is in factory/prompts/AGENT-XX.md.
   Writing outside it is a scope violation — stop and report to Master.

3. Status vocabulary is fixed. Use only:
     IMPLEMENTED = file/code exists
     TESTED      = automated test ran, output seen
     VERIFIED    = acceptance criterion from docs/PLAN.md confirmed
   Never use: done, complete, finished, passing, working, looks good.

4. Raw evidence only. Every Gate report must include raw terminal output —
   test runner logs with named test cases, raw SQL results, raw docker ps.
   Summaries are not evidence. "12/12 pass" without showing which 12 is not evidence.

5. No static credentials. Ever. For any agent, worker, or service.
   ADR-101-02. Non-negotiable.

6. No cross-tenant writes. Every tenant-scoped table has RLS.
   ADR and Section 7. Non-negotiable.

7. No policy engine bypass. Every task-graph node gets its own Permit decision.
   ADR-101-06. Non-negotiable.

8. No unregistered agents in production. Registry check = hard dependency.
   ADR-101-05. Non-negotiable.

9. Learning loop never writes directly. Only proposals/ output.
   ADR-101-11. Non-negotiable.

10. Voice has no elevated authority. Same Policy Engine path as API.
    ADR-101-12. Non-negotiable.

11. Console is locked. console/heer-console.html is a design spec.
    AGENT-12 wires it only. No restyling, no "cleanup".

## BEFORE SUBMITTING ANY GATE REPORT

Run the gate check and paste complete output:
  bash factory/gate-check.sh AGENT-XX

This does NOT replace Master review. It catches obvious gaps first.

## SESSION CORRUPTION PROTOCOL

If you experience 3+ tool call failures in a row, JSON parse errors,
git producing unexpected output, or inability to read files you just wrote:

  STOP. Do not try to recover in-place.
  Say exactly: "Session corrupted at [step] — requesting clean restart."
  Do not commit. Do not continue. Do not summarise what "should have worked."
  Wait for Master instruction.

## AMBIGUITY ESCALATION (in order)

1. docs/ARCHITECTURE.md — ADR question?
2. docs/PLAN.md — scope/acceptance question?
3. docs/ROADMAP.md — gate/ownership question?
4. factory/FACTORY.md — multi-agent coordination question?
5. Still unclear → stop, write the ambiguity clearly, tell Master.

"A reasonable assumption" is never acceptable on identity, tenant isolation,
policy evaluation, learning-loop write paths, or the console design.

## CURRENT FACTORY STATE

Check factory/DASHBOARD.md for authorized work packages.
Check factory/.pids/*.gate for which gates are accepted.
Do not start a work package whose dependencies are not gate-accepted.
